A||||3||btoth||RPMs drop when shifting (auto)||||4||
Z||000000||btoth||06-02-2001||10:21 PM||||When I shift out into Reverse from Park sometimes the idle drops to about 500 RPM and the engine stumbles until it kicks it back up to 900 RPM.  It does the same thing when shifting into Drive from Reverse or Park.  Once it kicks up the idle the car is just fine.  This wouldn't be much of a problem except that sometimes it stalls the engine... which is not good.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>* Brand new 2.8 in my auto '86 GT.  New kick-down cable and throttle cable.<P>Could it just be because the engine only has 300 miles on it or could my shift cable got bumped when the kick-down cable was installed?  Is there a way to check if the cable is not where it is supposed to be?<P>Thank you.||||reg||4
Z||000001||c m west||06-02-2001||11:36 PM||||I would check to see that the timing is correct and that the transmission has not been over or underfilled, possibly the Idle Air Control Valve- also check fuel pressure, and also a major concern is that the alternator is functioning up to spec - funny thing about these cars is that as soon as the alternator starts to go out, so will everything else<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000002||btoth||06-04-2001||11:00 AM||||I set the timing myself... 10 degrees, with the paperclip in the A & B holes of the diagnostic plug. (Done this many times in the past.)  This engine does have a performance cam in it.  I wish I knew the details on it... could this effect the usual timing setup?  The car idles fine and drives fine at all speeds when warm which is why I didn't consider it to be the timing.<P>All sensors have been replace by me before the new engine was installed.  Replace EGR valve a few months ago.  Replaced all vacuum lines with steel vacuum lines (no leaks).  The voltmeter on the dash is reading where it always reads... a notch above the 13.  (Except for the one day I drove through a massive puddle and soaked everything and the lights all dimmed in the car.  Problem?)<P>Just the other day I started getting really hard shifting while driving until the engine warms up and then it's fine.  (This is new and I haven't touched anything except for  cleaning my K&N filter.)<P>Possibilities maybe:  The shop replaced my 7.5mm wires with 7mm wires, thought about replacing them.  Should I replace the coil too maybe?  (It's a year old cheap one.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> )  Check plugs?  Could they not be gaped right?  Would it cause these symptoms?<P>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||84Bill||06-04-2001||11:41 AM||||Again as in previous posts regarding stumbeling, backfireing, spitting, hesitation problems. check the MAP sensor. Most of the time it is not reacting fast enough to the manifold pressure changes. The ECM sets the injector timing and pulse width based on bogus MAP information causing all kind of drivability problems.<BR>Check for cracks in the hose and general vacume leaks in the system<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-04-2001).]||||reg||14
